{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED on the SUPPORTED-HOST DECISION proposal 7b507ca7b81e4f3fca758924117a58cb9e3dd7d0. Design review at main 23c4c759, read-only, no head and no artifact. The direction is right and I endorse Option A: refusing an implied shared-host claim, keeping loopback-only listeners, and requiring an explicit operator acknowledgement is the honest description of what this codebase actually protects. The changes below are corrections to specific factual statements and to one internal contradiction, not a redirection.\n\nEVIDENCE CHECK, claim by claim.\n\n(a) \"gs serve accepts only TCP loopback\" - CONFIRMED with one gap. serveCommand validates before opening the workspace at cmd/gs/main.go:1662, and validateLoopbackListen at cmd/gs/main.go:1751-1764 refuses any host whose parsed IP is not loopback, with the reason naming the trusted-local-custodian assumption. The profiler listener takes the same check at cmd/gs/main.go:1725. residentclient.ValidateURL applies the mirror rule to clients at internal/residentclient/client.go:48-65. The gap: cmd/gs/main.go:1756-1758 returns nil for the literal hostname \"localhost\" without resolving it, so net.Listen at cmd/gs/main.go:1689 binds whatever the host's resolver returns. On a host whose hosts file or resolver maps localhost to a routable address, the refusal does not fire. The proposal makes \"keep non-loopback listeners refused\" a contract term, so it should say the check resolves the name and requires every resolved address to be loopback.\n\n(b) \"A client chooses an actor and a private session identifier in POST /v0/presence; the resident resolves that actor from repository custody and binds the identifier once; no client authentication\" - CONFIRMED. Route at internal/service/server.go:106. presenceRequest carries client-supplied Actor and Session at internal/service/server.go:361-368. handleAnnounce at internal/service/server.go:370-413 resolves the actor with workspace.Actor(input.Actor) and requires only that the actor name is configured and the session string is non-empty. Workspace.Actor at internal/app/app.go:512-519 reads the actor's ed25519 private key from the repository config key file. Binding is enforced per live lease at internal/nexus/nexus.go:317-328: an existing entry bound to a different actor or fingerprint is refused. There is no authentication of any kind: a repository-wide grep for Authorization, Bearer, token or api key headers across internal/service, internal/nexus, internal/app and cmd/gs returns nothing.\n\n(c) \"presenting it to /v0/say or /v0/act causes the resident to sign with that actor key\" - CONFIRMED. Speech: internal/service/server.go:520-538 resolves the session to an actor name with hub.SessionActor, fetches the private key through workspace.Actor, and passes it to hub.PublishMessageForSession. Durable acts: internal/service/ui.go:97-126 does the same and calls workspace.Act with the resolved actor name; workspace.Act at internal/app/app.go:709-719 loads that actor's private key and builds the signed kernel request. The comment at internal/service/ui.go:82-84 states the model plainly.\n\n(d) \"Public session handles are deliberately unrelated to the bearer identifier\" - CONFIRMED. mintHandle at internal/nexus/nexus.go:73-96 draws 16 bytes from crypto/rand and documents exactly the oracle argument the proposal summarises. Presence snapshots are keyed by handle, not identifier, at internal/nexus/nexus.go:652-659; HandleFor at internal/nexus/nexus.go:538-545 states there is no inverse; LiveSessionsForActor at internal/nexus/nexus.go:555-569 returns only a count, and its service caller at internal/service/server.go:324-335 says so.\n\n(e) \"expire on lease loss or resident restart\" - CONFIRMED. expire at internal/nexus/nexus.go:513-523 deletes any entry whose expiry has passed and drops its inbox; Depart at internal/nexus/nexus.go:495-505 removes it on request; the TTL is capped at two minutes with a 30 second default at internal/service/server.go:405-408. Restart: all presence is process-local map state created fresh in NewWithSigningKey at internal/nexus/nexus.go:265-276, and the hub is constructed per server at internal/service/server.go:77, so nothing survives a restart.\n\n(f) \"any local process able to reach the port can select any actor whose key the resident holds\" - CONFIRMED, and the service is more helpful to an attacker than the sentence suggests. GET /v0/actors at internal/service/ui.go:38-50 enumerates exactly the custodial identities the resident can sign for, unauthenticated, and its own comment calls them \"who a browser may join as\". The browser client picks one from that list and mints its own session at ui/src/lib/session.ts:16-20 and 30-40. Key files are per-actor 0600 files under the repository meta directory, written at internal/app/app.go:429-445 and read at internal/app/app.go:447-460, so the boundary really is the OS account.\n\nDEFECTS TO CORRECT.\n\n1. The compromise window is understated, and this is the one I would not merge without. The proposal says \"Exposure of a private session compromises that actor for the remaining lease\". Nothing binds renewal to the client that first announced the session. A holder of the identifier can re-announce it with the same actor at internal/service/server.go:370-411, which reaches announceFor at internal/nexus/nexus.go:330-388 and resets expiresAt to now plus ttl at internal/nexus/nexus.go:371. The guard at internal/nexus/nexus.go:324-326 only refuses a different actor. So exposure compromises that actor for as long as the holder keeps renewing, bounded only by resident restart or departure, not by the lease. The recovery paragraph already prescribes the right action (stop the resident, invalidate all sessions by restart), but the exposure sentence would tell an operator that a leaked session self-heals within two minutes. Say instead that a leaked identifier is renewable indefinitely by whoever holds it and ends only at departure or restart.\n\n2. The session-identifier hygiene rule contradicts what the code does today, and the record does not say so. The rule is that the identifier \"must remain out of presence, logs, URLs, diagnostics, and durable events\". Presence, logs and durable events hold: the identifier appears in no Go log statement, telemetry records the route template rather than the concrete path at internal/observe/http.go:47-49 and 65-81, and the durable payload built at internal/service/ui.go:120-124 carries only the resolved actor name. URLs and diagnostics do not hold. The departure route puts the private identifier in the path at internal/service/server.go:107 and 415-419, and both clients send it that way: ui/src/lib/api.ts:309 and cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1199. The MCP whoami tool returns the identifier to its client at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:809 and 828, advertised at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:545 as showing \"the ephemeral session\", which puts a live bearer credential into an agent transcript that is routinely logged and often leaves the host. The URL placement also arms a latent path: a transport failure on that call produces a url.Error carrying the full URL, wrapped as TransportError at internal/residentclient/client.go:229-231. The one caller discards it today, but Client.Delete at internal/residentclient/client.go:152-163 is exported, so the next caller that prints or wraps a transport error prints a live credential. The MCP concatenation at cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go:1199 also skips url.PathEscape, unlike the UI. Either record these as known exceptions with a repair obligation and a follow-up request, or the sentence reads as a description of the present state and is false. The hygiene list should also name adapter tool output and agent transcripts alongside logs and diagnostics.\n\n3. Criterion (9) sends a future reviewer to the wrong package. It lists \"custody\" as a boundary to verify, but internal/custody is an offer/accept/settle asset saga fold (internal/custody/custody.go:1-143) that holds no keys and is referenced only from the spike report. Actor key custody lives in internal/app: Config.Actors and Workspace.Actor at internal/app/app.go:512-519, key generation and reading at internal/app/app.go:429-460, and the custody flag surfaced at internal/app/app.go:36-47. The architecture page's own table row calling internal/custody \"manages local keys and migrations above the kernel\" is inaccurate against this main, which is how the proposal inherited the wrong name. Name internal/app and the key files, not internal/custody.\n\n4. The threat model is framed entirely as local processes and omits a client that is neither local nor a process: a hostile web page in a browser on this host can reach the loopback port. The good news is that this is already defended - guardMutation at internal/service/server.go:594-612 requires application/json on mutations, refuses a cross-origin Origin, and refuses a cross-site Sec-Fetch-Site - and that defence deserves to be named in the record, because a reader who takes \"the boundary is the OS account\" literally will not see why the browser case is covered, and a future implementer could remove the guard as redundant.\n\n5. Scope of the acknowledgement gate. The gate is specified for \"starting a signing resident\", but the resident is not the only wildcard signing path. Any process that can run the gs binary against the repository signs as any configured actor with no resident at all, through Workspace.Act at internal/app/app.go:709-719. Nothing auto-starts a resident, so this is a genuinely separate path. The record should say the acknowledgement discloses a trust assumption rather than enforcing a control, so that \"the resident is gated\" is never read as \"the host is safe\".\n\n6. One acceptance criterion is missing. Criterion (5) requires credentials that are unforgeable, but no criterion says the resident must mint the credential rather than accept one the client chose. The service imposes no entropy floor on that choice today, and internal/nexus/nexus.go:86-89 says so explicitly: the strength \"is only as strong as the weakest identifier any client ever chooses, and the service does not constrain that choice\". A shared-host implementation could satisfy (5) as worded while still accepting a client-supplied string. Add resident-minted, or an explicit entropy and shape requirement, to (5).\n\nJUDGMENT ON THE THREE QUESTIONS.\n\nEvidence-backed: yes for the six structural claims, which I verified individually above; no for the compromise-window sentence in defect 1 and the hygiene claim in defect 2.\n\nExplicit enough to prevent implied shared-host support: mostly yes. \"Starting a signing resident without an explicit trusted-local-clients acknowledgement must fail closed\" is a real fail-closed rule, and the sentence forbidding inference from loopback binding, a generic container, or file mode alone closes the three loopholes that would otherwise be taken. Two soft spots remain. The acknowledgement has no named form, so an implementer could satisfy it with a config default that ships pre-acknowledged; say that it must be an explicit per-invocation act by the operator or by automation that has to name it. And the gate covers only the resident, per defect 5.\n\nComplete: no, in the six ways above. Positively: the same-account malicious process is honestly declared out of scope, in the Option B paragraph and again in criterion (8), and that is the right call. Storage, revocation, restart invalidation and rotation are covered. The MCP surface is named in the deployment paragraph and in criterion (9), though defect 2 shows the concrete MCP leak that naming did not catch. Backup and copies of key material are not mentioned at all: the key files are ordinary 0600 files inside the git common directory, so any repository backup, clone-with-metadata, or editor sync carries every actor key off the trusted host. That belongs in the deployment boundary. Criterion (10) does not conflict with any other criterion; it constrains how Option B may be built, not what Option A must do.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers, using docs/reference/architecture.md: layer 3, nexus and live runtime, implemented by internal/nexus and hosted by internal/service; layer 7, the outer surfaces cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp, ui/ and the internal/service composition; and the application host and boundary adapter internal/app, which is where key custody actually sits. I verified the proposal's claim that no kernel ontology or signature rule changes: the sequencing kernel and internal/intent are untouched by anything the proposal requires, signing remains ed25519 over the canonical intent built at internal/app/app.go:721 onward, and the acknowledgement is a startup precondition in cmd/gs. That reading is correct. The proposal's own layer naming is not: \"application-host layer\" collides with layer 4, Application host binding, which is about interpreter selection and has nothing to do with transport trust or key custody. Name layers 3 and 7 and internal/app instead. No architecture page update is required by this record, which changes no code contract; the implementing head that adds the acknowledgement will change the gs serve contract and must update that page then.\n\nSECURITY. This review is the security analysis. The trust boundary is one OS account: loopback TCP with no client authentication, authority carried by a client-chosen bearer string, and a resident with read access to every actor key file in the repository. The proposal describes that boundary accurately and refuses to claim more, which is the correct outcome. Defences that exist and the record does not credit, and which should not be lost to a later simplification: the cross-origin and content-type guard at internal/service/server.go:594-612; the handle-to-identifier separation and its stated rationale at internal/nexus/nexus.go:73-96; the global and per-actor session caps at internal/nexus/nexus.go:336-347; the two-minute TTL cap at internal/service/server.go:405-408; inbox deletion on identifier reuse at internal/nexus/nexus.go:348-350, which stops a recycled identifier inheriting an earlier recipient's priority frames; and route-template-only telemetry at internal/observe/http.go:65-81, which is the reason the identifier in the departure URL does not also reach the collector. Unresolved defects blocking approval: the exposure window in defect 1, and the URL and diagnostic disclosure in defect 2.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Deferring Option B is genuinely the simpler choice and I would not weaken it: a local capability file readable by the same account defends against nothing that a same-account process cannot already do, so a partial authentication layer would add a framework and no property. Two pieces of the record are excess. Criterion (9) is a list of package names rather than a testable condition, and one of the names is wrong, per defect 3; cut it to independent security review of the resident, the adapters, and actor key custody, and let the reviewer find the packages. The recovery paragraph's nine-step chain restates what the durable practice already governs - retire, replace, rotate, never rewrite history - and could be two sentences: restart to invalidate every session, then treat the compromised principals as an ordinary retirement and replacement under normal authority. The load-bearing content is the refusal of a shared-host claim, the fail-closed acknowledgement, loopback only, the hygiene list once corrected, and criteria (1) through (8).\n\nCorrections 1 and 2 are the ones I consider blocking; 3 through 6 are small and specific. With those, I expect to approve."}